\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 In Chapter 3, Pel interrogates Mandri to make this determination.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I am having trouble motivating the scene in Chapter 2, in which Vigram introduces Pel to Mandri.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Currently, what happens is that, in Chapter 2, Mandri is giving Vigram a painting lesson, and Pel is the model. Vigram is painting, Mandri is teaching. This is where Mandri is introduced to Pel. Pel\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s presence feels unmotivated as she is there to merely observe, and the painting lesson itself is just a painting lesson.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Another element in chapter 2 is that an animal, an ox, interrupts Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s concentration by mooing. Vigram, quick to anger, narcissistic, rages at the ox and ends the lesson. Mandri intervenes by offering to buy the ox. Pel is intrigued by this act, as it shows that animal suffering disturbs Mandri, which is a quality she believes is necessary for the Mazid that she seeks.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 But in all, the core conflict in ch2 feels hazy. There is Pel \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91observing\u8217\'92 Mandri. Mandri believes he is there simply to make some money for a painting lesson. Vigram is hoping Pel sees what she needs to see in Mandri. And then there\u8217\'92s this random ox interrupting the scene just to provide some conflict.}
